00:02:05.880 --> 00:02:07.480
Part 1: Distract yourself

00:02:07.820 --> 00:02:10.400
A key way to cope with the anguish
of a meaningless world

00:02:10.400 --> 00:02:12.120
is to simply distract yourself.

00:02:12.120 --> 00:02:14.640
Naomi Watts: "I just wanted to do something 
light and fun"

00:02:14.640 --> 00:02:19.280
"to distract me from the deep well of sadness that is my life."

00:02:19.280 --> 00:02:22.300
Princess Carolyn persistently distracts
herself with her job

00:02:22.300 --> 00:02:25.460
and her compulsive need to
fix the life of those she cares about.

00:02:25.540 --> 00:02:27.880
Despite complaining about work dominating her life

00:02:27.880 --> 00:02:30.560
she can’t actually handle the free time
that comes without it.

00:02:30.560 --> 00:02:32.140
Lora: "Are you gonna head out soon?"

00:02:32.140 --> 00:02:33.200
Princess Carolyn: "Where else would I go?"

00:02:33.200 --> 00:02:34.440
When her agency folds

00:02:34.440 --> 00:02:37.640
she finally has time for the relationship
she always wanted

00:02:37.640 --> 00:02:41.040
but still finds a way to get right back to the
Hollywoo grindstone.

00:02:41.040 --> 00:02:42.460
Princess Carolyn: "I think I'm having an epiphany."

00:02:42.460 --> 00:02:44.320
"I'M GONNA BE A MANAGER!"

00:02:44.320 --> 00:02:44.940
Ralph: "Oohh.."

00:02:44.940 --> 00:02:50.060
Todd, BoJack’s best friend and perpetual mooch is happy as long as he has something to do

00:02:50.060 --> 00:02:51.960
Todd: "Hooray! A task!"

00:02:51.960 --> 00:02:54.360
The one time we do see Todd without anything to do

00:02:54.360 --> 00:02:57.380
he immediately starts to spiral into existential despair.

00:02:57.380 --> 00:02:58.580
Todd: "I need something to do."

00:02:58.580 --> 00:03:01.160
"A job or a task or a direction in life."

00:03:01.160 --> 00:03:02.480
"Do I have a purpose?"

00:03:02.480 --> 00:03:04.840
before finding Something to take his mind off it.

00:03:04.840 --> 00:03:07.400
Todd may not have much direction or common sense

00:03:07.400 --> 00:03:09.280
but he manages to keep himself busy:

00:03:09.280 --> 00:03:10.760
He composes a space opera

00:03:10.760 --> 00:03:12.100
builds his own Disneyland

00:03:12.100 --> 00:03:13.800
and starts a number of businesses.

00:03:13.800 --> 00:03:15.140
"An all hallow's eve store..."

00:03:15.140 --> 00:03:16.280
"...in January?"

00:03:16.280 --> 00:03:17.640
"How fiendishly droll."

00:03:17.760 --> 00:03:20.160
"Nuhhhhhhhhhhhhhh

00:03:20.160 --> 00:03:20.660
*crash*

00:03:20.660 --> 00:03:24.700
Similarly, Mr. Peanutbutter spends his life
in a state of distraction.

00:03:24.700 --> 00:03:26.420
Though he may seem like a moron

00:03:26.420 --> 00:03:29.100
BoJack: "He's so stupid, he doesn't realize how miserable he should be."

00:03:29.100 --> 00:03:31.480
Mr. Peanutbutter is more self aware than most.

00:03:31.480 --> 00:03:34.240
Mr. Peanutbutter: "The universe is a cruel uncaring void."

00:03:34.240 --> 00:03:36.740
"The key to being happy isn't the search for meaning."

00:03:36.740 --> 00:03:40.200
"It's to just keep yourself busy 
with unimportant nonsense."

00:03:40.200 --> 00:03:41.120
"And eventually..."

00:03:41.120 --> 00:03:42.040
"...you'll be dead."

00:03:42.040 --> 00:03:43.220
And then there’s BoJack.

00:03:43.220 --> 00:03:45.900
BoJack’s whole life is a series of distractions.

00:03:45.900 --> 00:03:48.540
The way BoJack spends his days is so trivial

00:03:48.540 --> 00:03:51.000
that the show often makes a joke of not showing us

00:03:51.000 --> 00:03:52.500
what he’s doing half the time.

00:03:52.500 --> 00:03:53.200
BoJack: "Yeaaah!"

00:03:53.200 --> 00:03:54.200
"Two days!"

00:03:54.200 --> 00:03:55.980
"I can do all those things."

00:03:55.980 --> 00:03:57.760
*two days later*

00:03:57.920 --> 00:03:58.840
BoJack: "Oh shit!"

00:03:58.840 --> 00:04:00.280
"I didn't do any of those things."

00:04:00.280 --> 00:04:02.300
In fact, in the years since "Horsin' Around"

00:04:02.300 --> 00:04:04.200
BoJack has been able to drift through life

00:04:04.200 --> 00:04:06.220
in a near constant state of distraction.

00:04:06.220 --> 00:04:07.340
Princess Carolyn: "Horsin' Around?"

00:04:07.340 --> 00:04:08.960
"That ended 11 years ago."

00:04:08.960 --> 00:04:09.500
BoJack: "Really?"

00:04:09.500 --> 00:04:10.580
"Where does the time go?"

00:04:10.580 --> 00:04:11.600
*drinks to forget*

00:04:11.600 --> 00:04:12.660
But in the first episode

00:04:12.660 --> 00:04:15.940
BoJack has a “mild anxiety attack” that
sends him to the hospital.

00:04:15.940 --> 00:04:18.320
The doctor tells him he needs to 
take it easier.

00:04:18.320 --> 00:04:20.100
Princess Carolyn: "Take it easy!? Are you kidding?"

00:04:20.100 --> 00:04:24.520
"He doesn't have a job he has no real responsibilities he doesn't do anything but take it easy."

00:04:24.520 --> 00:04:27.500
The show asks a question commonly directed
at celebrities

00:04:27.500 --> 00:04:30.540
They have everything, so how could they not be happy?

00:04:30.540 --> 00:04:32.480
BoJack: "Great house, great career, 'great life' "

00:04:32.480 --> 00:04:34.480
"That must be why I'm so goddamn happy all the time."

00:04:34.480 --> 00:04:38.240
This question was addressed by 17th Century philosopher Blaise Pascal

00:04:38.240 --> 00:04:41.600
who is considered by many to be a 
precursor to the Existentialists.

00:04:41.600 --> 00:04:44.100
For Pascal, when given too much time to think

00:04:44.100 --> 00:04:46.920
humans will eventually contemplate
their own insignificance

00:04:46.920 --> 00:04:48.800
which inevitably bums them out.

00:04:48.800 --> 00:04:49.980
Or as Pascal puts it:

00:04:49.980 --> 00:04:54.120
“I have discovered that all the unhappiness
of men arises from one single fact:

00:04:54.120 --> 00:04:56.520
that they cannot stay quietly in their own chamber.

00:04:56.520 --> 00:04:58.820
I have found that there is one very real reason

00:04:58.820 --> 00:05:01.940
namely, the natural poverty of our feeble
and mortal condition

00:05:01.940 --> 00:05:05.320
so miserable that nothing
can comfort us when we think of it closely.”

00:05:05.440 --> 00:05:08.840
For Pascal, humans have a natural psychological
defense mechanism

00:05:08.840 --> 00:05:10.500
that keeps these thoughts at bay:

00:05:10.500 --> 00:05:12.540
We’re really easily distracted.

00:05:12.540 --> 00:05:15.700
And when we’re distracted we stop thinking about our existence

00:05:15.700 --> 00:05:18.540
and can be, if not happy at least functional.

00:05:18.540 --> 00:05:21.540
Pascal directly addressed the
unhappy celebrity question

00:05:21.540 --> 00:05:24.020
though he uses kings instead of movie stars:

00:05:24.020 --> 00:05:27.400
“Yet, when we imagine a king attended with
every pleasure he can feel

00:05:27.400 --> 00:05:31.680
if he be without diversion and be left to consider 
and reflect on what he is

00:05:31.680 --> 00:05:34.060
this feeble happiness will not sustain him.”

00:05:34.180 --> 00:05:35.000
Like a king

00:05:35.000 --> 00:05:37.940
BoJack has no serious struggle to occupy his life.

00:05:37.940 --> 00:05:41.360
Instead, he has to spend all of his time trying
to distract himself

00:05:41.360 --> 00:05:44.440
so that he never has to think about the
reality of his situation.

00:05:44.440 --> 00:05:46.800
Although he succeeds in doing this for years

00:05:46.800 --> 00:05:48.600
the whole charade comes crashing down

00:05:48.600 --> 00:05:50.140
when he reads Diane’s book

00:05:50.140 --> 00:05:51.280
“One Trick Pony.”

00:05:51.280 --> 00:05:54.040
In the book, BoJack sees an honest reflection of himself

00:05:54.200 --> 00:05:55.080
warts and all.

00:05:55.080 --> 00:05:58.420
He is forced to face all the things he really is and isn’t

00:05:58.420 --> 00:06:01.040
and to confront the extent of his self-deception.

00:06:01.040 --> 00:06:03.140
"I love the part about how you became famous."

00:06:03.140 --> 00:06:05.520
"You could be constantly surrounded by distraction"

00:06:05.520 --> 00:06:06.860
"and wouldn't have to be alone with yourself."

00:06:06.860 --> 00:06:10.240
So what should BoJack do 
now that his distraction has crumbled?

00:06:10.240 --> 00:06:14.520
For Pascal, once you’ve passed the critical point where diversion will no longer sustain you

00:06:14.520 --> 00:06:16.740
the only recourse is to turn to God.

00:06:16.740 --> 00:06:18.960
But for BoJack this isn’t an option.

00:06:18.960 --> 00:06:21.980
It seems that Hollywoo is a post-God-Is-Dead world.

00:06:21.980 --> 00:06:25.200
In the Christmas Special, the show uses Santa Claus 
as a stand-in

00:06:25.200 --> 00:06:27.340
to discuss the non-existence of God:

00:06:27.340 --> 00:06:29.500
BoJack: “He’s a lie that grown ups made up because"

00:06:29.500 --> 00:06:32.060
"we like to believe that there’s an order to the universe"

00:06:32.060 --> 00:06:34.060
"and that good behavior will lead to happiness"

00:06:34.060 --> 00:06:34.860
"but the fact is"

00:06:34.860 --> 00:06:36.200
"that just isn’t true.”

00:06:36.240 --> 00:06:37.780
But if there’s no God

00:06:37.780 --> 00:06:39.460
where are we supposed to turn?

00:06:39.460 --> 00:06:42.660
For BoJack, the first choice is
right back to distraction.

00:06:42.660 --> 00:06:44.780
The show provides two separate
case studies

00:06:44.780 --> 00:06:46.980
of what happens when you try to force distraction

00:06:46.980 --> 00:06:48.580
after the illusion has been broken

00:06:48.580 --> 00:06:50.120
and neither ends well.

00:06:50.120 --> 00:06:54.040
First, Secretariat gives a young BoJack advice
that basically amounts to

00:06:54.040 --> 00:06:55.040
“Don’t Think About It.”
*same as Rick Sanchez*

00:06:55.040 --> 00:06:55.720
Secretariat: "BoJack."

00:06:55.720 --> 00:06:57.860
"When you get sad...you run..."

00:06:58.140 --> 00:06:58.920
"straight ahead"

00:06:58.920 --> 00:07:00.780
"and..and you keep running forward"

00:07:00.860 --> 00:07:01.760
"no matter what."

00:07:01.960 --> 00:07:04.420
"There are people in your life
who are gonna try to hold you back"

00:07:04.420 --> 00:07:06.520
"slow you down but you don't let them."

00:07:06.520 --> 00:07:08.640
"Don't you stop running and don't you ever look behind you."

00:07:08.640 --> 00:07:11.140
"There's nothing for you behind you."

00:07:11.140 --> 00:07:13.620
"All that exists is what's ahead."

00:07:13.620 --> 00:07:17.420
But once he’s disqualified from racing and
can no longer keep the thoughts at bay...

00:07:17.420 --> 00:07:22.940
"You can be the fastest runner in the world, but you can't outrun the truth."

00:07:23.140 --> 00:07:24.800
The second case is Sarah Lynn.

00:07:24.800 --> 00:07:28.920
BoJack drags Sarah Lynn on a months long bender that only ends when she OD’s

00:07:28.920 --> 00:07:30.280
on well BoJack

00:07:30.280 --> 00:07:31.560
(the heroin, not the horse).

00:07:31.560 --> 00:07:33.860
Looking back on it that might have been a bit on the nose.

00:07:33.860 --> 00:07:37.740
If trying to reclaim his distracted state
is doomed to end in tragedy

00:07:37.740 --> 00:07:40.000
what other options does BoJack have?

00:07:40.280 --> 00:07:41.900
Part 2: Radical Freedom

00:07:42.120 --> 00:07:44.760
From the beginning, BoJack rejects responsibility:

00:07:44.880 --> 00:07:46.360
“I’m responsible for my own happiness?"

00:07:46.360 --> 00:07:48.280
"I can’t even be responsible for my own breakfast.”

00:07:48.460 --> 00:07:51.880
He wants to escape the realities
of his own terrible life choices.

00:07:51.880 --> 00:07:54.140
BoJack: "I didn't do anything wrong because I can't do anything wrong."

00:07:54.140 --> 00:07:55.620
"EVERYTHING IS MEANINGLESS!"

00:07:55.620 --> 00:07:58.080
"NOTHING I DO HAS CONSEQUENCE!"

00:07:58.080 --> 00:08:00.660
After his first bender, BoJack asks Diane:

00:08:00.660 --> 00:08:02.700
"Am I just doomed to be the person that I am?"

00:08:02.700 --> 00:08:03.880
"The person person in that book?

00:08:03.880 --> 00:08:06.440
"I know that I can be selfish and narcissistic
and self destructive"

00:08:06.440 --> 00:08:08.060
"but underneath all that, deep down"

00:08:08.060 --> 00:08:10.760
"I'm a good person and
I need you to tell me that I'm good."

00:08:10.760 --> 00:08:15.040
BoJack is afraid of what 20th Century French
philosopher Jean-Paul Sartre called

00:08:15.040 --> 00:08:16.180
“Radical Freedom”

00:08:16.180 --> 00:08:18.300
which was a major focus of his philosophy

00:08:18.300 --> 00:08:19.380
Existentialism.

00:08:19.380 --> 00:08:21.200
BoJack: "I stand by my critique of 'Sartre'."

00:08:21.200 --> 00:08:25.640
"His philosophical arguments helped many 
tyrannical regimes justify over-cruelty."

00:08:25.740 --> 00:08:27.140
"Also the French smell and I hate them."

00:08:27.140 --> 00:08:29.880
If Existentialism had a catchphrase, it would be:

00:08:29.880 --> 00:08:31.740
“Existence Precedes Essence.”

00:08:31.740 --> 00:08:35.160
An object’s essence, for Sartre, can be thought of as its purpose.

00:08:35.160 --> 00:08:38.520
If I make a hammer, I create it with a particular purpose in mind

00:08:38.520 --> 00:08:41.680
i.e. hammering, so we would say that the hammer’s essence

00:08:41.680 --> 00:08:43.460
precedes its existence.

00:08:43.460 --> 00:08:45.900
For a Christian a human is like a hammer

00:08:45.900 --> 00:08:49.000
a tool created by God with a purpose in mind.

00:08:49.000 --> 00:08:52.340
But for an Existential Nihilist, humans, and horsemen

00:08:52.360 --> 00:08:54.460
exist just because.

00:08:54.460 --> 00:08:57.140
Unlike a hammer or a Gentle Farms chicken

00:08:57.140 --> 00:09:01.960
there’s no purpose underlying BoJack’s existence and nothing he is meant to do.

00:09:01.960 --> 00:09:05.780
There is nothing stopping BoJack from stealing
the D from the Hollywood sign

00:09:05.780 --> 00:09:08.900
or doing three months worth of drugs, and on the flip side

00:09:08.900 --> 00:09:11.420
nothing forcing him to do those things either.

00:09:11.580 --> 00:09:14.860
BoJack has total control over every choice he makes.

00:09:14.860 --> 00:09:16.180
This is what Sartre meant by

00:09:16.180 --> 00:09:17.260
“Radical Freedom”

00:09:17.260 --> 00:09:19.560
and it's definitely not supposed to be comforting:

00:09:19.560 --> 00:09:22.860
“That is what I mean when I say that man
is condemned to be free."

00:09:22.860 --> 00:09:25.460
"Condemned, because he did not create himself,"

00:09:25.460 --> 00:09:27.100
"yet is nevertheless at liberty,"

00:09:27.100 --> 00:09:29.540
"and from the moment that he is thrown into this world"

00:09:29.540 --> 00:09:31.900
"he is responsible for EVERYTHING he does.”

00:09:31.900 --> 00:09:33.900
Or, as Diane and BoJack put it:

00:09:33.900 --> 00:09:36.980
Diane: “That’s the thing, I don’t think I believe in 'deep down'."

00:09:36.980 --> 00:09:39.660
"I kinda think all you are is 
just the things that you do.”

00:09:39.780 --> 00:09:40.780
BoJack: "Well that's depressing."

00:09:40.780 --> 00:09:43.080
There is no “good BoJack” deep down.

00:09:43.080 --> 00:09:44.780
There is only the one BoJack

00:09:44.780 --> 00:09:48.300
and he is entirely defined by the choices
he's made so far.

00:09:48.340 --> 00:09:51.360
Or, as Todd puts it to BoJack at the end of season 3:

00:09:51.360 --> 00:09:54.060
"You are all the things that are wrong with you."

00:09:54.060 --> 00:09:58.300
While this might not seem too comforting it does offer BoJack one bit of hope:

00:09:58.300 --> 00:10:01.880
Yes, BoJack is defined by the choices
he's made so far

00:10:01.880 --> 00:10:04.680
but he is not “doomed to be the person in that book” forever.

00:10:04.680 --> 00:10:07.800
He has the freedom to change his actions and himself.

00:10:07.800 --> 00:10:10.120
But it’s entirely up to him to do so.

00:10:10.120 --> 00:10:13.120
"It's never too late to be the person you want to be."

00:10:13.240 --> 00:10:14.980
"You need to choose the life you want."

00:10:14.980 --> 00:10:17.500
The show provides an optimistic take on Sartre

00:10:17.500 --> 00:10:20.820
by using water as a metaphor for
BoJack’s Radical Freedom.

00:10:20.820 --> 00:10:23.680
BoJack often finds himself
overwhelmed by water.

00:10:23.680 --> 00:10:26.540
But at the end of the underwater episode in Season 3

00:10:26.540 --> 00:10:29.080
after falling from the ledge of the taffy factory

00:10:29.080 --> 00:10:32.000
BoJack finally recognizes the water for what it is

00:10:32.000 --> 00:10:34.100
not an overwhelming and scary force

00:10:34.100 --> 00:10:36.740
but instead a medium of incredible opportunity.

00:10:36.900 --> 00:10:38.980
Part 3: Embrace The Absurd

00:10:38.980 --> 00:10:43.240
When Mr. Peanutbutter’s brother gets sick he goes through his own existential crisis:

00:10:43.240 --> 00:10:46.280
"Come to work, clock in, you put sugar in your coffee"

00:10:46.280 --> 00:10:49.220
"and watch it slowly disappear into nothingness."

00:10:49.220 --> 00:10:52.220
"But the sugar doesn’t know why. Sugar didn’t ask to be born."

00:10:52.220 --> 00:10:54.180
But he doesn’t suffer
through it for very long.

00:10:54.180 --> 00:10:57.040
"I realized something today,
when my brother called from the hospital."

00:10:57.040 --> 00:10:59.220
"And I think it's gonna make you feel a lot better."

00:10:59.220 --> 00:11:00.100
BoJack: "What? What is it?"

00:11:00.100 --> 00:11:02.060
Mr. Peanutbutter: "None of this matters!"

00:11:02.060 --> 00:11:06.840
BoJack gets similar advice from his
old writing partner and Harvard alum Cuddly Whiskers

00:11:06.840 --> 00:11:08.420
who abandoned his Hollywoo life

00:11:08.420 --> 00:11:10.600
Cuddly: "An Oscar is meaningless. It's all meaningless."

00:11:10.600 --> 00:11:12.600
and is finally able to find happiness.

00:11:12.600 --> 00:11:17.420
"Only after you give up everything can you begin to find a way to be happy."

00:11:17.420 --> 00:11:20.880
Mr. Peanutbutter’s and Cuddly Whisker’s
paths to happiness after

00:11:20.880 --> 00:11:23.400
or even because of the realization of nihilism

00:11:23.400 --> 00:11:26.640
invokes the 20th century French thinker Albert Camus

00:11:26.640 --> 00:11:28.600
and his philosophy of Absurdism.

00:11:28.600 --> 00:11:31.700
For Camus, the universe is irrational and meaningless

00:11:31.700 --> 00:11:35.180
and yet humans are desperate to find
reason and meaning in it.

00:11:35.180 --> 00:11:37.820
And he called this discrepancy “The Absurd”.

00:11:37.820 --> 00:11:41.060
Once you become aware of “The Absurd”, you only have three choices:

00:11:41.060 --> 00:11:44.760
Return to the cycle of daily life and don’t think about it, commit suicide,

00:11:44.820 --> 00:11:47.280
or, his preferred option rebellion

00:11:47.280 --> 00:11:50.060
i.e., accept the absurd and be happy anyway,

00:11:50.060 --> 00:11:52.840
because screw the universe
for thinking it can control how you feel.

00:11:53.020 --> 00:11:56.580
Camus illustrates these ideas in his essay ,The Myth of Sisyphus,

00:11:56.580 --> 00:11:59.600
about a man who is doomed to push a rock up a hill each day

00:11:59.600 --> 00:12:01.840
only to watch it roll back down again.

00:12:01.840 --> 00:12:04.580
Sisyphus becomes Camus’s "Absurd Hero"

00:12:04.580 --> 00:12:07.100
when he recognizes the absurdity of his task

00:12:07.100 --> 00:12:09.320
but decides to be happy in spite of it.

00:12:09.320 --> 00:12:11.820
The show directly references The Myth of Sisyphus.

00:12:11.820 --> 00:12:13.580
Princess Carolyn: "I have to push a boulder up a hill"

00:12:13.580 --> 00:12:16.140
"and then have it roll over me time and time again"

00:12:16.140 --> 00:12:20.380
There’s also a recurring symbol of the Baboon
jogging up the hill outside BoJack’s house.

00:12:20.380 --> 00:12:22.620
Every day, he’s running up that same hill.

00:12:22.620 --> 00:12:23.820
"It get's easier."

00:12:23.820 --> 00:12:24.320
BoJack: "Huh?"

00:12:24.320 --> 00:12:26.100
Baboon: "Everyday it gets a little easier."

00:12:26.100 --> 00:12:26.600
Bojack: "Yeah?"

00:12:26.600 --> 00:12:28.220
Baboon: "Butcha gotta do it everyday."

00:12:28.480 --> 00:12:29.480
"That's the hard part."

00:12:29.480 --> 00:12:30.840
At the end of Season Three

00:12:30.840 --> 00:12:33.920
BoJack comes face to face
with the meaningless loop of his life

00:12:33.920 --> 00:12:35.960
in the young actress from “Ethan Around”.

00:12:35.960 --> 00:12:37.320
Sarah: "I wanna be like you."

00:12:37.320 --> 00:12:38.440
BoJack: "Like me?"

00:12:38.520 --> 00:12:39.780
Sarah: "I wanna be famous."

00:12:39.780 --> 00:12:40.660
BoJack: "Oh no."

00:12:40.720 --> 00:12:43.080
“It happens that the stage set collapses..."

00:12:43.080 --> 00:12:46.440
"At the end of the awakening comes, 
in time, the consequence: "

00:12:46.440 --> 00:12:48.180
"Suicide or recovery.”

00:12:48.180 --> 00:12:51.580
Just as Camus describes BoJack runs out of the stage set

00:12:51.580 --> 00:12:53.480
and begins to drive into nowhere.

00:12:53.480 --> 00:12:58.120
Driving has repeatedly served as a way for
BoJack to try to escape the absurd

00:12:58.120 --> 00:13:00.580
maybe his own take on Secretariat's advice to

00:13:00.580 --> 00:13:02.040
“run and don’t look back.”

00:13:02.040 --> 00:13:04.020
But when he releases the steering wheel

00:13:04.020 --> 00:13:07.880
BoJack gives in submitting himself to the Absurd and to suicide.

00:13:07.880 --> 00:13:10.080
However, when he sees the Wild Horses

00:13:10.080 --> 00:13:12.140
they represent for him the alternative:

00:13:12.140 --> 00:13:15.320
Embrace the absurd carry out your meaningless task

00:13:15.320 --> 00:13:16.940
and be happy in spite of it.

00:13:16.940 --> 00:13:20.320
They may be seen as absurd heroes a group of horses,

00:13:20.320 --> 00:13:22.940
a group of horses who have embraced running 
purely for its own sake.

00:13:22.940 --> 00:13:27.280
Like Sisyphus, they run carry out the meaningless task and are happy anyway.

00:13:27.280 --> 00:13:30.300
BoJack has proven himself willing to give everything up.

00:13:30.300 --> 00:13:32.560
Maybe he is ready to follow Cuddly Whisker’s advice

00:13:32.560 --> 00:13:34.760
and can finally start to become happy.

00:13:35.200 --> 00:13:35.900
Conclusion

00:13:35.900 --> 00:13:39.780
The show does offer one possible solution
to BoJack’s struggle for meaning:

00:13:39.780 --> 00:13:40.840
Family.

00:13:40.840 --> 00:13:43.360
BoJack: "At our age, it's weird if you don't have a family."

00:13:43.360 --> 00:13:44.040
"I mean..."

00:13:44.040 --> 00:13:45.420
Charlotte: "Well...not for you."

00:13:45.420 --> 00:13:46.660
Bojack: "No."
Charlotte: " 'Cause you've been busy..."

00:13:46.660 --> 00:13:47.800
BoJack: "Right"
Charlotte: "...with your career."

00:13:47.800 --> 00:13:48.300
BoJack: "Sure."

00:13:48.300 --> 00:13:51.540
When you pay attention to the all the times
BoJack watches “Horsin Around”

00:13:51.540 --> 00:13:54.200
the scenes all revolve around the in-show family.

00:13:54.200 --> 00:13:56.180
BoJack as "The horse": "I've learned that when you're in a loving family"

00:13:56.180 --> 00:13:57.680
"there's nothing wrong with a little"

00:13:57.740 --> 00:13:59.340
"horsin' around."
*get it?....it's the name of the show*

00:13:59.660 --> 00:14:01.120
During the first big bender

00:14:01.120 --> 00:14:03.440
he has a vision of himself as a simple family man.

00:14:03.440 --> 00:14:06.160
BoJack’s attempt to sleep with Penny
at the end of Season 2

00:14:06.160 --> 00:14:08.600
illustrates how desperate he is to start over

00:14:08.600 --> 00:14:11.460
and choose the domestic life
he could have had with Charlotte.

00:14:11.680 --> 00:14:14.800
The Wild Horses may represent more 
than just the "Absurd Hero"

00:14:14.800 --> 00:14:16.540
they may also represent the herd

00:14:16.540 --> 00:14:18.980
and a sense of belonging that BoJack lacks.

00:14:18.980 --> 00:14:20.880
And with the teaser at the end of season three

00:14:20.880 --> 00:14:24.520
it seems the show is preparing to explore
this theme of family much more.

00:14:24.580 --> 00:14:26.100
Though I hope it works out for him

00:14:26.100 --> 00:14:27.880
something tells me that even family

00:14:27.880 --> 00:14:29.580
won’t be able to fill the void.

